Z||000017||Steven||06-11-2001||06:23 PM||||Engine and trans are bolted togather, and I now have to shuffle the bolts and studs around to put them in the correct location. The Eng/trans are bolted to the cradle via the new mounts. The real problem are all the things that I had not planned on. Like having to buy a new distributor. I had wanted to rebuild it, but the housing was bad, so off to the parts store I went. I like the design of the new dist. better. Instead of the six finger like things on the shaft and the six seating on top of the pick up. This new one has a six pointed star on the shaft, and the piece on top of the pick up that stays stationary has six points. I will take a pic and post it later on tonight. My alt also lock up on me while it was sitting. All goes well, I hope to start and break in the cam by Thursday.||||reg||1
Z||000018||ThatRickGuy||06-11-2001||06:58 PM||||88formula, by that pic looks like you are running those pistons almost out of the hole, and w/ a thin head gasket. are the valves gonna clear? Anyway, looks great man! wish I had gone your route instead of the 3.2||||reg||1
Z||000019||88formula||06-11-2001||08:26 PM||||ThatRickGuy,<P>The piston is .010-.012” lower than the top of the deck. I originally had less but because I had to have the rods reconditioned I ended up with more. The reason why I am looking for a .035 head gasket or less is because I am trying to keep the quench area below .050". If I can get a .035" head gasket than I will have .045-.047".<P>With a quench area of more than .050”- .060” you forgo the benefits of the quench head design and could end up with detonation. I also dulled any sharp edges in the combustion chamber like the deck ridges and combustion chamber edges so that they won’t get red hot and self ignite the fuel. <P>I always check piston to valve clearance.   <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Shaun41178||06-11-2001||09:07 PM||||Those pistons look like they have the same dish as the 3.1 pistons that people are using.  only thing that would be different are the bore sizes.  hmm someone was saying a compression ratio of 8.2:1 with the 3.1 pistons.  if the dish is the same and the only thing different is the bore size I doubt the bore size would drop the compression ratio that much.  But I have been wrong before.  <P>hey GT GT 87 (steve) i hope you see this and could maybe get a pic up of the 3.1 pistons I sold you to compare.  or just look at the pic and eyeball it and tell us what you think<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Shaun41178||06-11-2001||09:14 PM||||Also i would like to comment on the drilling holes in the pistons.<P>i cound't tell where the holes were drilled but I can tell you that drilling holes in pistons is a old hot rodders trick<P>But it also matters where you put them.  The hot rodder way is to drill tiny tiny holes in the top of the piston out near the edge of the piston and to take them as deep as the first ring land.  You want so space the holes evenly around the piston.  This does two things.  first it lightens the piston.  Second when the gas ignites and burns and expands in the chamber, it finds these little holes in the piston and when it goes down into them it pushes the top ring out towards the cylinder wall helping to keep the gasses in the combustion chamber where they belong reducing blowby which in turn increases horsepower.<P>now like I said I don't know where the holes were drilled but perhaps this is what he was doing?  <P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000022||88formula||06-11-2001||10:13 PM||||The holes were drilled deep into the pin boss weakening the piston. I had a few professionals look at them for me and they basically laughed and said what I already knew. Here is a closer look.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/holes_in_piston.jpg">    <P><BR>You can't tell what the dish size is by looking at a picture! Been though the compression ratio argument already. Go here and see <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008285.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008285.html</A> <P>Here is a picture of the polished combustion chambers fitted with SI valves. The valves are undercut to increase low lift flow. The intake seat has three angles and the exhaust seat has a radius.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/combustion_chambers.jpg">  <P>Here is a picture of the polished exhaust ports. They are like mirrors.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/exhaust_port_polished.jpg">     <P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||Brucepts||06-11-2001||10:34 PM||||Drilling holes in the top of the piston is called gas porting, no noticable gain will be found on the dyno but you will go through top rings alot faster! For a daily driver its a no-no!!<P>Must say he did "butcher" your pistons!!! That was alot of weight to try and remove there.<P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||13
